          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "**Please get this checked by a doctor soon.** The tool that checks for emergency patterns rated your description \"routine,\" but that rating is not a clearance. It only matches a fixed list of presentations. A squeezing pressure behind the breastbone that comes on with exertion and eases with rest is a pattern that clinicians want to assess promptly.\n\n**Call emergency services now** if the feeling happens at rest, lasts more than a few minutes, or comes with sweating, shortness of breath, nausea, fainting, or pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back. Do not wait to see if it passes.\n\nOn your second question, the reference material I searched did not cover this. The passages I retrieved were about osteoarthritis exercise, reflux and diet, COPD activity, and medical coding. None of them described angina, its causes, its urgency, or how it is prevented. I can't tell you what guidance says about prevention from these sources. A clinician can explain what might be causing your symptoms and what steps would suit you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
